A||N||6||fierocd||v-6 engine change||||1||
Z||000000||fierocd||02-26-2001||09:28 PM||||I recently bought an 86gt with a bad engine.  I have an 86se v-6 with a good engine but various other problems.  I'm am going to start to change the engine and was wondering if anyone had some helpfull tips to help me out.  I have already done an engine swap on my 84, I was able to take that engine out from the top and was wondering if anyone knows if you can do that with the v-6.||||reg||1
Z||000001||99BlackZ||02-26-2001||09:53 PM||||I did this swap a few years back.<BR>had an 85se with a bad engine. Bought an 85GT that was totaled with good engine.<BR>Just make sure you have plenty of time.<BR>The only trouble I had was the water pipe that attaches to the front side of the engine was slightly different between the 2 and I had to use the old one.  No biggie.  They might be the same on 2 86's.<BR>If it is a 4 or 5 sp, you should go ahead and do the clutch, pressure plate and throw out bearing while you have it apart. Just to be on the safe side.  Replace any exhaust manifold bolts with new quality pieces.  Those suckers are notorious for breakage.  Put new plugs in it too.  You know, all the crap that is hard to do while the engine is still in the car.  I didn't attempt doing it from the top.  I did it from the bottom.  Wasn't that bad.   Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||02-27-2001||04:05 PM||||Yes you can swap engines from the top.  You still have alot of underneath things to get at though, that just are not accessible from the top.  I've taken engines out both ways and both have pros & cons.  Taking the eng out (with the cradle) from the bottom is not as bad as it seems.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierocd||02-27-2001||04:21 PM||||The reason I was worried about taking it out from the bottom was to get the back of the car lifted up that high to get the engine and cradle out.  I wasn't sure exactly how to get it up that high and hold it there.  <P>The good engine I have already has a new clutch, pressure plate, throwout bearing, and shaved flywheel.  I put them on less than a week before I crashed the car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||02-27-2001||04:35 PM||||Sounds alot easier to swap cradles than to swap motors...<P>alot of jacking and jackstands and 4x4's or cinder blocks can get the back end up high enough to remove the entire cradle<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Jan1617s.jpg">   <BR>Our lady of blessed acceleration dont fail me now!<p>[This message has been edited by Greg Piet (edited 02-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierocd||02-27-2001||07:54 PM||||one other question I thought of, when I was looking in my haynes manual, it said something about a support fixture to hold the engine up.  does anyone know what I can use for this.  When I did my clutch I used an engine hoist, but I don't know if I can get one again.||||reg||1
Z||000006||pontiacs4me||02-27-2001||08:25 PM||||when I did my engine swap, I dropped both cradles.  I did it with a pallet, floor jack, two big jack stands, and a 8X8 block of wood, about 18" long.  I disconnected all the stuff under the deck lid, loosened the carriage bolts and took off the wheels.  I lowered the car so the carriage would rest on the pallet.  I took the rest of the carriage bolts out and jacked up each side with the jack, and put a stand under each, I put them in the notched area at the front of the rear subframe mounting pad, where the frame mount meets the floor pan.  Then I took the floorjack out, and placed the 8x8 block on it.  I placed the jack back under the car with the block positioned between each side of the floor pan to the rearmost part of the gas tank.  It is a good place to jack from as the floor pans are strong there because of structrual bending of the pans.  Be very careful when you jack it up.  You may want to put some weight in the front of the car, ( I used 2 100 lb. bags of plaster.)  And definately block the front wheels.  So jack it up and pull the pallet out.  You may want a friend or two to help you.  I did it by myself, but it takes alot of stregnth, I just think of Christina Aguilera.  Hey it worked!.  <BR>Ok now youve swapped cradles or engines whatever and your ready to put it back in.  Heres my tip.  Put it back in the same as you took it out just in reverse order, but when aligning the carriage bolts do this, take the jackstands out and lower the car back down to the pallet.  Take an engine hoist and put the hook on the trunk latch bar.  Have a friend lower it slowly as you and another friend watch the front mounting areas with a flashlight or something.  With the hoist connected at the trunk latch bar you can push the car around easy to align it. Much easier than trying to get a floor jack to slide across the garage floor.  Doing this wont damage the latch bar, at least it didnt damage mine.  So lower it down start the carriage bolts by hand, tighten them up, lower the hoist, jack it back up and put it back on jackstands.  Now you are ready to hook everything back up.  I did this last week to my 87, I had about 45 total hours in this project, and I had to take an engine off one transmission and put it on another.  Thats hard.  Anyway if you are just swapping cradles it wont take near the time to complete.  good luck.||||reg||1
